# TODO - Working Blooket writes and browser fallback

## Objective

Validate actual action payloads and responses, select supported transports
before mutation, and reconcile ambiguous outcomes without replay.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when the product can create a set and add or edit admitted
questions through a working Blooket transport, with the resulting quiz verified
from fresh remote state. A browser implementation is enough to ship this
functionality. HTTP writes are an optional optimization: do not postpone a
working browser path to reverse-engineer an unverified private HTTP response.

Select any supported transport before mutation. If a write might have happened,
reconcile before retrying or using another transport; never create duplicates
merely because a request timed out. Demonstrate actual product-owned Create Set
and Add Question behavior plus focused failure tests. Leave speculative HTTP
coverage and broad compatibility exploration to ongoing hardening.

## Implementation notes

The candidate helper still supports only text create/add. Tests pin the observed
update set/question action hashes as identifiers only; those hashes do not
implement editing, and no HTTP write is activated by these request fixtures.

Implement concrete write surfaces using the session/read adapters from task 08.
Confirm authentication, current build, account capabilities, and the exact
payload before a mutation; preserve every human-stop state.

Prefer HTTP only for an admitted, currently verified operation. Use the browser
fallback for unsupported operations before sending a mutation. A timeout or
ambiguous primary result must enter journal reconciliation, never an immediate
fallback or retry that can duplicate a quiz/question.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-08, authorized Chrome creation of a private fictional development
set confirmed two visible Add Question buttons on both empty and nonempty
Edit pages. The lower button shares its immediate parent with the unique
Show all answers control; the header button does not. The previous global
uniqueness assumption prevented both question writes and capability probing.

The page runners now resolve only that observed list toolbar when exactly two
Add Question buttons exist. Unknown duplicate layouts still fail rather than
choosing the first button. Synthetic regressions reproduce the old refusal,
confirm one toolbar click, and reject duplicate Add Question controls within
the toolbar. The browser-created fictional multiple-choice question retained
its text, answers, and 15-second limit after reload; this is UI observation,
not canonical product CLI/MCP publication acceptance.

On 2026-10-08, the Create Set browser host added a post-submit route
check to reject acknowledgments from an unrelated tab destination. It still
permits a legitimate in-progress Edit redirect, but requires separate stable
read-back before reporting the new set. Synthetic tests cover both outcomes.

On 2026-10-08, Create Set preparation and submission began requiring one
unique form and one copy of each scoped title, description, and privacy
control. Duplicate forms and values outside the intended form now fail
before editing controls or clicking the submit button. The synthetic DOM
fixture covers ambiguous forms and unscoped controls, not live creation.

On 2026-10-08, Create Set redirect confirmation began requiring two
identical decoded receipts from the same owned Edit URL, separated by a
browser event-loop yield. Synthetic tests reject a missing second receipt,
a changed payload, and a route change during confirmation without converting
a one-off browser response into a publication receipt.

On 2026-10-08, the local browser broker began reserving a write-dispatch
margin for both Create Set and Add Question, matching the existing capability
cleanup budget. Queued writes with less than 9.5 seconds left on the default
ten-second deadline are settled without dispatch, rather than beginning a
form action whose caller may already have timed out. Injected-clock tests
cover refusal and a fresh singly dispatched write.

On 2026-10-08, Create Set redirect confirmation began requiring two matching,
strictly decoded Edit-page results separated by an event-loop yield. A route
change, malformed second reply, or contradictory remote ID now refuses success
rather than treating the first browser observation as a durable receipt. These
are synthetic browser-host checks, not live evidence of remote persistence.

On 2026-10-08, the extension worker started passing a generation-scoped
browser port to each write host. A synthetic reconnect during an Add Question
modal opener now prevents all later form scripts, including submission, and
blocks an obsolete response from reaching the new workspace. Browser API
calls already in flight cannot be undone, so this is a continuation safeguard
rather than atomic ownership or live acceptance.

On 2026-10-08, the Create Set Chrome host began requiring its observed
response ID to match the unique decoded `id` query field on the confirmed
Edit-page redirect. Extra reply fields, non-string IDs, ambiguous duplicate
queries, and a response claiming another set now fail closed, while opaque
identifiers with encoded spaces and slashes remain admitted. This hardens
synthetic read-back evidence but does not verify remote persistence.

On 2026-10-08, the Add Question read-back host began decoding complete
question candidates through the versioned IR contract and requiring two
matching observed values before confirming a submitted question. Malformed
number lists and unexpected reply fields cannot authorize a read-back modal;
uncertain or mismatched content triggers best-effort modal cancellation with
no mutation replay.

Synthetic tests confirm changing question contents, malformed envelopes, and
cleanup after a mismatched read fail closed. These checks do not establish a
live Blooket action response, authenticated persistence, or a remote receipt.

On 2026-10-08, the Add Question Chrome host added exact edit-route ownership
checks around its opener, readiness, preparation, one submit, and each later
read-back script. A navigation between the initial tab check and requested
edit page no longer overwrites a user-selected route. Synthetic regressions
cover stale navigation, modal opening, preparation, and read-back without a
live mutation.

On 2026-10-08, the synthetic Create Set Chrome host added a fresh tab-route
check before navigation so an independently selected edit route is not
overwritten. Exact Create-page checks now guard form preparation and submission;
late preparation replies from another route fail rather than enabling a write.

A claimed Edit-page redirect is also checked after its browser script completes
before reporting its observed set ID. Three portable regressions reproduced the
previous stale navigation, preparation, and redirect success paths. None of
these tests performs a live Blooket mutation or verifies an HTTP action body.

The current observed Blooket build is
`4e10e84779aaa361fd4310c02366e37ebee7b60d`. Keep this evidence distinct from
older captures in `reference/curated/`. Use the recovered references according
to `AGENTS.md`; never import their code into production.

Observed actions include create set, add question, and update question. Create
posts multipart fields to `/create`; add/update post to `/edit` with the set
context. The initial Flight form state is `UNSET` with message and field errors.
The private creation checkbox is omitted for a private quiz; its UI label is
inverted relative to the field name, so do not infer visibility from the name.

Sanitized synthetic payload fixtures now pin title/description, set ID, question
JSON, answer order/correctness, `mc`/`typing`, match modes, seconds, and the
inverted public/private checkbox semantics. Media-backed set/question candidates
remain unsupported before any HTTP mutation.

The real action response body was not recovered: attempts to retrieve it through
the browser tool failed. HTTP 200 and UI read-back are not a decoded response
contract. Capture and validate actual response shapes before activating HTTP
writes, and never treat `SUCCESS` inferred from client code as observed
evidence.

Bounded Flight candidate decoding was hardened on 2026-10-06 against the
recovered production runtime. IDs, JSON rows, UTF-8 byte-length `T` rows,
duplicate IDs, row/graph limits, direct `$<hex>` references, shared references,
cycles, and unsupported reference/tag kinds are now explicit and fail closed.

Action-state status is type-checked without coercion, field errors are bounded,
and malformed JSON is rejected rather than replaced with null. `E` records
expose only their bounded digest. The same-build Next action reducer establishes
that redirects arrive through `x-action-redirect`, so redirect metadata is
decoded separately and restricted to same-origin HTTPS instead of being invented
as a Flight row. Synthetic tests cover all of these cases.

Same-build action response metadata now has an explicit candidate decoder:
`text/x-component` must match exactly before Flight parsing, redirects remain
same-origin HTTPS, and revalidation flags preserve their wire-level zero/one
values. HTTP status, `SUCCESS`, redirects, revalidation, and error digests are
returned only as response evidence; the candidate shape has no confirmation or
receipt field.

Pre-mutation transport selection is also explicit. While the HTTP response
contract remains unverified, even lowerable HTTP payloads select the browser
before mutation. Unsupported HTTP operations also select browser before
mutation, while any already-ambiguous primary mutation requires reconciliation
and cannot fall back or retry.

The browser fallback now has a concrete text-only Create Set path. Its page
helper recognizes only the observed create form, uses native control setters,
revalidates the exact values immediately before one submit click, and accepts
only the observed same-origin /edit?id=... success navigation.

The Chrome host preserves session/challenge states and bounds navigation
polling. Its internal sets.create bridge command accepts only bounded
title/description/privacy fields; media, arbitrary fields, malformed surface
results, and Add Question remain fail-closed.

The local browser-service composition routes this bridge surface through the
canonical browser write execution adapter, so an observed remote set ID is
decoded again before it can become a durable receipt. No external publication
command is registered yet, and no live application-driven Create Set acceptance
has been claimed from these synthetic transport tests.

A private one-question quiz was created through the authorized Blooket UI and
changed from multiple choice to typing with a 15-second limit. Its final type
and time persisted after reload. Request fields were observed; the action
response body and application-driven publication were not verified.

The private test quiz remains in the account; do not delete it automatically or
commit its ID/account content. Temporary captures and logs may be cleaned
between sessions. The durable conclusions are recorded here; do not assume an
ignored fixture still exists. The configuration regression log is currently
under `.temp/readme-ui-check/full-tests.log`.

## Blockers

Acceptance remains open until the checks above run through the actual owning
product surfaces; portable foundations alone do not complete this record.
